\docType{package}
\title{RGL-Compatible rmetal API}
\usage{
open3d(...)
close3d(dev = cur3d(), ...)
cur3d(...)
set3d(dev = cur3d(), ...)

shade3d(x, ..., tag = NULL)
wire3d(x, ..., tag = NULL)
points3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, ..., tag = NULL)
lines3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, ..., tag = NULL)
segments3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, ..., tag = NULL)
triangles3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, ..., tag = NULL)
quads3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, ..., tag = NULL)
text3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, texts, ..., tag = NULL)
spheres3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, radius = 1, fastTransparency = TRUE, ..., tag = NULL)
planes3d(a, b = NULL, c = NULL, d = 0, ..., tag = NULL)

view3d(theta = 0, phi = 15, fov = 60, zoom = 1,
       scale = par3d("scale"), interactive = TRUE, userMatrix,
       type = c("userviewpoint", "modelviewpoint"))
par3d(..., no.readonly = FALSE, dev = cur3d(),
      subscene = currentSubscene3d(dev))
scene3d(minimal = TRUE)
ids3d(type = "all", tags = NULL, subscene = NA, ...)
tagged3d(tags, ...)
pop3d(id = NULL, type = "shapes", ...)
clear3d(type = "shapes", ...)

mesh3d(x, y = NULL, z = NULL, vertices, material = NULL,
       normals = NULL, texcoords = NULL, points = NULL,
       segments = NULL, triangles = NULL, quads = NULL,
       meshColor = c("vertices", "edges", "faces", "legacy"))
tmesh3d(vertices, indices, homogeneous = TRUE, material = NULL,
        normals = NULL, texcoords = NULL,
        meshColor = c("vertices", "edges", "faces", "legacy"))
qmesh3d(vertices, indices, homogeneous = TRUE, material = NULL,
        normals = NULL, texcoords = NULL,
        meshColor = c("vertices", "edges", "faces", "legacy"))

rmetal_show(scene = scene3d(), file = .rmetal_viewer_file(),
            launch = interactive())
rmetal_scene_json(scene = scene3d(), pretty = TRUE)
rmetal_save_scene_json(file, scene = scene3d(), pretty = TRUE)
rmetal_viewer_path()
rmetal_viewer_available()
rmetal_backend()
rmetal_compatibility_status()

create_rgl_compat_package(path)
use_rmetal_in_package(path)
}
\description{
The \pkg{rmetal} package exposes an \pkg{rgl}-style API backed by a
Swift/RealityKit viewer for macOS. The current implementation is focused
on the drawing, scene inspection, selection, and lifecycle calls needed by
Lithics3D, while keeping names for wider \pkg{rgl} compatibility.
}
\arguments{
\item{...}{Additional arguments accepted for \pkg{rgl} compatibility. Only
documented rmetal material fields such as \code{color}, \code{col},
\code{alpha}, \code{size}, \code{lwd}, and \code{radius} affect rendering
unless otherwise noted.}
\item{dev}{Scene/device id. rmetal currently manages a single active scene
for typical Lithics3D workflows.}
\item{x, y, z}{Coordinates, a coordinate matrix/data frame, or a
\code{mesh3d} object depending on the function.}
\item{tag, tags}{Object tag or tags used by \code{tagged3d()} and
\code{pop3d()}.}
\item{texts}{Text labels for \code{text3d()} and \code{texts3d()}.}
\item{radius}{Sphere radius or material radius.}
\item{fastTransparency}{Accepted for \pkg{rgl} compatibility; currently
ignored.}
\item{a, b, c, d}{Plane coefficients for \code{planes3d()}.}
\item{theta, phi, fov, zoom, scale, interactive, userMatrix, type}{View
parameters stored in \code{par3d()} and used by the RealityKit viewer where
implemented.}
\item{no.readonly}{Accepted for \pkg{rgl} compatibility. rmetal does not
currently distinguish read-only parameters.}
\item{subscene}{Accepted for \pkg{rgl} compatibility. Subscene support is
partial and maps to the single active scene.}
\item{minimal}{Whether to request the compact scene representation.}
\item{id}{Object id or ids returned by drawing functions.}
\item{vertices}{Mesh vertices, usually as a 3-by-n or 4-by-n matrix.}
\item{material}{Optional material metadata stored on mesh objects.}
\item{normals, texcoords}{Accepted and stored where present; advanced normal
and texture processing is not implemented.}
\item{points, segments, triangles, quads}{Optional mesh topology indices.}
\item{meshColor}{Accepted for compatibility with \pkg{rgl} mesh
constructors.}
\item{indices}{Triangle or quad index matrix.}
\item{homogeneous}{Whether input vertices are already homogeneous.}
\item{scene}{Scene object, normally from \code{scene3d()}.}
\item{file}{Path to a scene JSON file.}
\item{launch}{Whether \code{rmetal_show()} should launch the live viewer.}
\item{pretty}{Whether JSON output should be formatted with whitespace.}
\item{path}{Package source path for migration helpers.}
}
\details{
Use \code{rmetal_compatibility_status()} for the authoritative compatibility
table. It returns one row per exported compatibility function with one of
three statuses:

\itemize{
\item \strong{implemented}: supported by the rmetal scene store and, where
  relevant, by the live RealityKit viewer.
\item \strong{partial}: present for source compatibility, but only a subset
  of \pkg{rgl} behavior is implemented. Examples include simplified
  decorations, subscene helpers, projection helpers, placeholder mesh
  processing, and viewer-selection helpers.
\item \strong{not implemented}: a compatibility stub. These functions warn
  and return \code{invisible(NULL)}.
}

Implemented drawing primitives include mesh shading via \code{shade3d()},
wireframes via \code{wire3d()}, points, lines, line segments, triangles,
quads, text, spheres, planes, object ids, tags, scene JSON export, and the
basic viewer lifecycle. Materials currently support color, alpha, point or
line size, and sphere radius. Advanced \pkg{rgl} material features, WebGL
widgets, shader management, animation controls, and file writers are not
implemented.

The RealityKit viewer is compiled lazily from the Swift source in
\file{inst/swift}. Static scene tests are useful for structure and JSON
serialization, but rendered-scene behavior should be verified with a live
viewer process and its \file{<scene-file>.viewer.log}.
}
\value{
Drawing functions return \code{rglId}-like integer ids invisibly or visibly
following \pkg{rgl} conventions. Scene inspection functions return lists or
data frames. \code{rmetal_compatibility_status()} returns a data frame with
columns \code{function_name}, \code{status}, and \code{notes}. Unsupported
compatibility stubs return \code{invisible(NULL)} after warning.
}
\section{Installation from GitHub}{
Install from \url{https://github.com/cornelmpop/rmetal} with:

\preformatted{
install.packages("remotes")
remotes::install_github("cornelmpop/rmetal")
}

The package requires macOS 13 or later, R 4.2 or later, Apple's Swift
compiler and SDK frameworks from Xcode or the Xcode Command Line Tools,
and RealityKit. Use \code{swiftc --version} to confirm that the compiler is
available before first use. The live viewer is compiled lazily when a drawing
call first needs it for a cache location.
}
\examples{
open3d()
mesh <- tmesh3d(
  vertices = t(matrix(c(0, 0, 0,
                        1, 0, 0,
                        0, 1, 0), ncol = 3, byrow = TRUE)),
  indices = matrix(c(1, 2, 3), nrow = 3),
  homogeneous = FALSE
)
shade3d(mesh, color = "green", alpha = 0.5)
scene <- scene3d()
close3d()

head(rmetal_compatibility_status())
}
\keyword{package}
